      en: "The Dundas Centre for the Performing Arts is the premier cultural hub for theater and music in Nassau. Established in 1930 through the initiative of Lady Dundas, the wife of the then-Governor Sir Charles Dundas, the site originally operated as a vocational training school. By the 1960s, the facility was repurposed into a theater and rapidly became the heart of the Bahamian performing arts scene. It provides a vital platform for local actors, playwrights, and musicians, frequently hosting prestigious events such as the Bahamas National Youth Choir performances and local drama festivals. The architecture focuses on theatrical functionality and acoustics to enhance the intimacy of performances. The Dundas Centre remains a cornerstone for the preservation of Bahamian cultural identity through the arts and continues to foster the creative development of the nation's youth.",
